<section class="source-text source-copy project-section" style="--source-space:96px;--source-height:170px" data-source-section="1477:3060"><div data-node-id="1477:3060" data-name="Case Block / The System, Flexing" class="content-stretch flex gap-[60px] h-[170px] items-start overflow-clip relative shrink-0 w-[1344px]"><div data-node-id="1477:3061" data-name="Label" class="content-stretch flex flex-col h-[170px] items-start overflow-clip relative shrink-0 w-[330px]"><p data-node-id="1477:3062" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-bold font-bold leading-[1.6] relative shrink-0 text-[#0a0a0a] text-[length:12px] tracking-[0.96px] uppercase w-[300px]">ART DIRECTION</p></div><div data-node-id="1477:3063" data-name="Copy" class="content-stretch flex flex-col h-[170px] items-start overflow-clip relative shrink-0 w-[954px]"><p data-node-id="1477:3064" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-normal leading-[1.5] relative shrink-0 text-[#0a0a0a] text-[length:22px] w-[930px]">Art direction is often mistaken for choosing a visual style. Here it meant defining the rules behind every image and interaction: casting, crop, light, colour, movement, pacing, product behaviour and the relationship between editorial atmosphere and product truth. The work only looks simple once those decisions have been made.</p></div></div></section>
